cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Laguna Genealogies: A Digital Edition of Parsons 1923"
message: >-
  If you use this edition, please cite both it and the 1923 publication it
  transcribes (see references).
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Heger-Vlahovic
    given-names: Elizabeth
abstract: >-
  A verified digital transcription of the genealogical plates published with
  Elsie Clews Parsons's "Laguna Genealogies" (1923). Three of the four plates
  are edited here, 452 individuals in all. Table 1, "Genealogy I", records 104
  individuals across five generations, with 27 marriages and 80 parent-child
  links. Table 2, "Genealogy II", records 275 individuals across six
  generations, with 61 marriages and 214 parent-child links, in three descent
  blocks. Table 4, "Genealogy IV", records 73 individuals across four
  generations, with 14 marriages and 58 parent-child links. Table 3 is not yet
  edited. All are transcribed character by character including Americanist
  phonetic diacritics, and encoded as a normalised persons/unions/children
  schema. Clan descent is matrilineal, which gives a structural check on the
  bracket readings, though only where the candidate mothers belong to different
  clans; elsewhere the evidence is the plate's own bracket geometry.
keywords:
  - Laguna Pueblo
  - genealogy
  - Elsie Clews Parsons
  - Keresan
  - digital edition
  - Southwest anthropology
license: CC-BY-4.0
# The CONCEPT doi, which always resolves to the newest version. A citation made
# against it keeps working when Table 3 is added; the per-release doi is
# under identifiers for anyone who needs to pin a specific version.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21637900
# These two describe the newest release that ACTUALLY EXISTS on Zenodo -- never
# a planned one. GitHub renders this file as the repo's "Cite this repository"
# widget, so a version here that has not been minted advertises something no
# reader can resolve. They said v1.1.0 / 2026-07-30 until the release policy was
# set on 2026-07-30 (see CLAUDE.md): during active development the project
# commits to main and cuts NO releases and NO Zenodo deposits, and the next
# release comes only when all four tables, the design, the transcriptions, the
# text and the citations are final. Do not bump these in anticipation of a tag.
#
# The abstract above deliberately describes MORE than this release contains: it
# describes the work, these fields describe the release.
version: v1.0.0
date-released: "2026-07-28"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21637900
    description: Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest version
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21637901
    description: Version DOI — v1.0.0, Genealogies I and IV
  # The next version DOI will not exist until Zenodo's webhook fires on the
  # release, which under the current policy is not until the edition is
  # complete. Add it here afterwards; do not guess it from v1.0.0's, the suffix
  # is not sequential in any way that can be relied on.
repository-code: "https://github.com/PuebloGenealogy/pueblogenealogy.github.io"
url: "https://pueblogenealogy.github.io/"
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Laguna Genealogies"
    authors:
      - family-names: Parsons
        given-names: Elsie Clews
    year: 1923
    journal: "Anthropological Papers of the American Museum of Natural History"
    volume: "19"
    issue: "5"
    start: 133
    end: 292
    publisher:
      name: "American Museum of Natural History"
    location:
      name: "New York"
